The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
final HashSet<Integer> testSet = Sets.newHashSet();
testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
```
Reason:
The `testSet` is accessed by multiple threads without synchronization, which can lead to concurrent modification issues when multiple threads attempt to add codec identity hash codes simultaneously. This can result in inconsistent or incorrect data being stored in `testSet`.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
synchronized (testSet) {
    testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
}
```
Reason:
By wrapping the `add` operation in a `synchronized` block, access to `testSet` is controlled, ensuring that only one thread can modify it at a time. This prevents concurrent modification issues and maintains the integrity of the data stored in `testSet`.

